    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you go into more detail about the enzyme itself? like, how does the lisinopril actually interact with the ACE enzyme to stop it from working? i'm trying to visualize the binding process.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lisinopril acts as a competitive inhibitor that physically occupies the active site of the Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE). It mimics the structure of the natural substrate (Angiotensin I) but binds with higher affinity, preventing the enzyme from completing the peptidyl-dihydropeptidase reaction. Because lisinopril itself is not a substrate, the reaction with Angiotensin I cannot occur, halting the production of Angiotensin II and leaving the enzyme free but inactive in a process called drug-induced inhibition.",
      "source": "target",
